# ── Production Data Setup ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This file lets you run the frontend locally but hit the PRODUCTION backend.
# Useful for design/dev work with real data — no local backend needed.
#
# HOW IT WORKS:
#   The production backend sets session cookies with Domain=.parqlet.com.
#   Browsers only send those cookies to parqlet.com subdomains (never localhost).
#   Solution: add dev.parqlet.com → 127.0.0.1 in /etc/hosts (or Cloudflare DNS).
#   Then Caddy proxies dev.parqlet.com → localhost:3000 (your Next.js dev server).
#   The browser sends .parqlet.com cookies to dev.parqlet.com → session works!
#
# SETUP STEPS:
#   1. DNS: add dev.parqlet.com → 127.0.0.1
#      Cloudflare: Type A, Name dev, Content 127.0.0.1, Proxy OFF
#      Or /etc/hosts: 127.0.0.1 dev.parqlet.com
#
#   2. Backend CORS: add "https://dev.parqlet.com" to CORS_ORIGIN on the VM.
#      On the VM: sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/parqlet-api.service.d/override.conf
#      or edit the env_file entry in docker-compose.yml on the VM, then:
#      docker compose down && docker compose up -d
#
#   3. Terminal 1 — start Caddy (download from https://caddyserver.com):
#      caddy run --config Caddyfile.local
#
#   4. Terminal 2 — start Next.js dev server:
#      npm run dev
#
#   5. Open: http://dev.parqlet.com  (NOT localhost!)
#      Sign in with any production account.
#
# IMPORTANT: dev.parqlet.com must resolve to YOUR machine. Without DNS/hosts entry
# the browser will fail to connect. Caddy must be running on port 80/443.

NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://dev.parqlet.com
